  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nOvernight showers and a few thunderstorms continue early this morning\nas a warm and moist air mass lingers over the Ohio Valley. Additional\nthunderstorms will form this afternoon as temperatures rise and\ninstability increases. While a few strong wind gusts cannot be ruled\nout with the tallest storms, weak wind shear and slow storm motions \nwill more likely favor the potential for locally heavy rainfall over \nany damaging wind threat. As outflow boundaries form from collapsing\nupdrafts, light southerly flow may continue to feed instability \nnorthward, enhancing the locally heavy rainfall threat. Both the SPC \nHREF and REFS give the highest potential for this to occur across \nsouthern Ohio, southeast Indiana and northern Kentucky. The threat is\nnot widespread enough to warrant a watch, but areas that receive \nmultiple thunderstorms could see enough rain to cause flooding. \n\nThere will likely be a lull in thunderstorm activity during the\novernight hours, but a trough approaching from the northwest results\nin additional thunderstorm chances starting Saturday mid-morning and\ninto the afternoon. Activity will shift southeastward throughout the\nafternoon and into the evening. \n\nThe big picture jet stream pattern begins to evolve on Sunday, with a\nstalled boundary lifting northward. Some thunderstorms will be\npossible, but the overall forcing will be limited by the trough\nshifting to the east. The best chances for thunderstorms are late in\nthe afternoon and into the evening as peak heating is achieved. \n \nBetter moisture begins to move back into the Ohio Valley Sunday into \nMonday. A longwave trough over Hudson Bay in Canada provides a \nfairly steady period of persistent northwesterly upper level flow \nover the Midwest and Great Lakes regions. In the lower atmosphere, \nsouthwesterly flow will feed moisture northward supporting higher \nvalues of instability across the corn belt. As mentioned in SPC's 4-7\nday severe weather outlook, this type of synoptic regime will \nsupport the potential for more organized thunderstorm complexes \nsomewhere across the region. Until confidence increases for either \nday, the finer details on the potential for damaging winds Monday \nthrough Wednesday will have to wait. \n\nBy mid to late week, confidence is increasing that drier air from \nthe trough to the north will shift southward into the Ohio Valley, \nbringing cooler and drier conditions for the late part of the work \nweek.",
